# Database Rules (Drizzle ORM)

## Why Drizzle

- TypeScript-first: schema in `.ts` files, not a DSL.
- SQL-first: explicit control over queries (important for fitness analytics).
- No heavy code generation. Lightweight runtime.
- Better domain model separation than Prisma.

## Schema Rules

- Location: `src/infrastructure/database/schema/`.
- Table names: snake_case, plural (`workout_sessions`).
- Column names: snake_case (`created_at`).
- Primary keys: `id` UUID.
- Timestamps: `timestamptz`, always UTC.
- Foreign keys: always explicit.

## Repository Pattern

- One repository per aggregate root.
- Repositories implement Application port interfaces.
- Repositories map Drizzle rows ↔ Domain objects.
- No business logic in repositories.
- Repositories are the ONLY place that imports Drizzle.

## Query Rules

- Use explicit `select()`. No over-fetching.
- Avoid N+1 queries. Use joins or `inArray()`.
- Use transactions for multi-step mutations.
- Index foreign keys and frequently queried columns.

## Constraints

- NOT NULL on required fields.
- UNIQUE on natural keys.
- CHECK for value ranges (RPE 1-10, weight > 0, reps > 0).
- FOREIGN KEY on all relationships.

## Migrations

- Generated by `drizzle-kit`. Never hand-edit.
- One migration per logical change.
- Backward-compatible migrations preferred.

## Error Handling

- Catch DB errors at repository level.
- Translate to application errors.
- Never leak Drizzle/PostgreSQL errors to use cases or UI.

## Forbidden

- ❌ No Drizzle imports in React components.
- ❌ No Drizzle imports in domain/application layers.
- ❌ No business logic in SQL queries.
- ❌ No `select: true` over-fetching.

See `docs/database.md` for full details.